⚪ **Severity: LOW**

If `cudaMalloc` or `cudaMemcpyAsync` fails, the error goes unchecked and `h_flag` remains `0`. This causes `check_g_idx_in_bounds` to return `true`, silently bypassing the bounds check and allowing the main GEMM kernel to execute with unvalidated `g_idx` values.
Helpful? Add 👍 / 👎

<details>
<summary>💡 Fix Suggestion</summary>

**Suggestion:** Make the function fail-closed by checking the return values of CUDA API calls. At minimum, check `cudaMalloc` and return `false` on failure. Ideally, also check `cudaMemset`, `cudaMemcpyAsync`, and `cudaStreamSynchronize` return values, and return `false` if any of them fail. This ensures that if any CUDA operation fails, the function reports the bounds check as failed (fail-closed) rather than silently passing (fail-open). For example:

```cpp
bool check_g_idx_in_bounds(const int* g_idx, int n, int upper_bound) {
  int* d_flag = nullptr;
  if (cudaMalloc(&d_flag, sizeof(int)) != cudaSuccess) return false;
  if (cudaMemset(d_flag, 0, sizeof(int)) != cudaSuccess) { cudaFree(d_flag); return false; }
  // ... launch kernel ...
  int h_flag = 0;
  cudaError_t err = cudaMemcpyAsync(&h_flag, d_flag, sizeof(int), cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost, stream);
  cudaError_t sync_err = cudaStreamSynchronize(stream);
  cudaFree(d_flag);
  if (err != cudaSuccess || sync_err != cudaSuccess) return false;
  return h_flag == 0;
}
```

